[
  {
    "pkheadingid": 968,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Definition",
    "headingPosition": 1,
    "headingid": 733,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 139424,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 264,
        "headingId": 733,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Infantile haemangioma (IH) is a benign vascular tumour characterized by a rapid proliferative phase during infancy followed by gradual involution.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T15:07:23"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 969,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-O coding",
    "headingPosition": 2,
    "headingid": 734,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 139425,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 264,
        "headingId": 734,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>9131/0 Infantile haemangioma</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T15:07:23"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 970,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 735,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 139426,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 264,
        "headingId": 735,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>2E81.2Y &amp; XH3U29 Other specified benign vascular neoplasms of infancy or childhood &amp; Capillary haemangioma</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T15:07:23"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 971,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 736,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 139427,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 264,
        "headingId": 736,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Not recommended: </em>capillary haemangioma; juvenile haemangioma; strawberry naevus; capillary-cavernous haemangioma; infantile haemangioendothelioma.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T15:07:23"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 972,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 737,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 139428,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 264,
        "headingId": 737,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T15:07:24"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 973,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 738,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 139429,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 264,
        "headingId": 738,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>IH most commonly involves the skin of the upper eyelid&nbsp;/ orbit and less frequently the conjunctiva, sclera, and iris {28540013;21788081;34632621;22486338}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T15:07:24"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 974,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 739,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 139430,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 264,
        "headingId": 739,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>IH appears soon after birth as a distinct or diffuse red mass and undergoes rapid growth (up to 2&nbsp;years), with most tumours involuting by 48&nbsp;months {21788081;33069287}. Approximately 20% of children have additional non-periocular haemangiomas {22776927}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T15:07:24"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 975,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 740,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 139431,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 264,
        "headingId": 740,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>IHs have a female predilection and more common in White populations and premature infants {22776927}. This lesion accounts for 1&ndash;5% of all ocular adnexal tumours {15121380;27725148;30450316} [[Heegaard S, Grossniklaus H, editors. Eye Pathology: An Illustrated Guide. Heidelberg (Germany): Springer; 2014.]], 40&ndash;80% of all vascular eyelid tumours {26028084;30450316}, and 15&ndash;20% of vascular conjunctival tumours {21788081;27725148}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T15:07:24"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 976,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 741,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 139432,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 264,
        "headingId": 741,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The etiology of IH is not well understood. Germline genetic variants and familial clustering have been reported as risk factors {27940781}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T15:07:24"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 977,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 742,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 139433,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 264,
        "headingId": 742,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>IH is caused by the uncontrolled proliferation of clonally expanded vascular endothelial cells {11254674}. Causative genes have not been described. Hypoxia has been postulated to play a role in the pathogenesis of IH, supported by the demonstration of GLUT1, IGF2, VEGF-A, and HIF1&alpha; in endothelial cells {26940670;12032304;21079070}. Immunophenotypic similarity to placental microvessels has raised a theory that IH may result from embolization of placental cells or stem cells differentiating towards placental vascular cells {11346333}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T15:07:24"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 984,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 749,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 139434,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 264,
        "headingId": 749,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>IH usually occurs as a lobulated vascular-appearing mass.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T15:07:24"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 978,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 743,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 139435,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 264,
        "headingId": 743,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>IH in the proliferative stage is composed of capillary-sized vascular channels lined by plump endothelial cells intermixed with pericytes, arranged in lobules around larger vessels. In the regressive stages, the lobules are progressively replaced by fibrosis.</p>\r\n<h5>Immunohistochemistry</h5>\r\n<p>Endothelial cells express GLUT1, CD31, ERG, and CD34 and lack staining with D2-40 {24355066}.</p>\r\n<h5>Differential diagnosis</h5>\r\n<p>Congenital non-progressive haemangiomas and lobular capillary haemangioma (see related section) are distinguished from IH based on clinicopathological correlation and GLUT1 immunohistochemistry.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T15:07:24"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 979,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 744,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 139436,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 264,
        "headingId": 744,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T15:07:24"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 980,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 745,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 139437,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 264,
        "headingId": 745,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T15:07:24"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 983,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 748,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 139438,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 264,
        "headingId": 748,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> proliferative stage: lobules of capillaries each of which is surrounded by a layer of pericytes; regressive stage: fibrous tissue replacing capillary lobules.</p>\r\n<p><em>Desirable:</em> endothelial cells are GLUT1-positive.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T15:07:24"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 981,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 746,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 139439,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 264,
        "headingId": 746,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T15:07:24"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 982,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 747,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 139440,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 264,
        "headingId": 747,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>IH involutes spontaneously; some lesions can be associated with amblyopia from compression of the eyeball and obscuration of vision. Eyelid lesions respond to oral and topical propranolol {22967870;21810027}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T15:07:25"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]